1
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:11,652
Phil Bernstein as our speaker today.

2
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:17,348
Dr. Bernstein is a distinguished scientist at Microsoft Research.

3
00:00:17,934 --> 00:00:24,228
He has published more than 150 papers and two textbooks, two books, all related to database systems.

4
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:31,748
He is a world-class expert on many different topics, especially database transaction processing.

5
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,240
He has a long list of awards.

6
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:40,708
To name a few, he's an ACM Fellow, a recipient of the ACM Sigmod COD Innovation Award.

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:54,148
One thing I want to mention is Dr. Bernstein has a very high reputation for his very constructive professional comments when he reviews conference papers.

8
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:58,320
especially I noticed that when I was chair for some conference.

9
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:05,600
another thing I want to mention is Dr. Bernstein also influences some of our own work at the UCI.

10
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:12,772
for example, my team has been building an open source system called Texera to do parallel data analytics.

11
00:01:13,198 --> 00:01:20,420
And our decision of choosing the ACT model was partially based on a conversation I had with Dr. Bernstein a few years ago.

12
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:24,196
So without further ado, let's welcome Dr. Bernstein.

13
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:28,800
Great, thank you, Chen, for the very nice introduction.

14
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:39,440
so I'm here to give you a retrospective on a project that I spent many years on and called Orleans.

15
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:47,524
And I'm gonna start though with a um my slides are not, there we go.

16
00:01:48,400 --> 00:02:06,180
I'm gonna start with a kind of a meta discussion about database research in the systems area and just how I go about doing it and how I think what what I think is really driving the agenda, since I understand the entire audience here is our database people.

17
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,320
Um so what do you do?

18
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:20,932
Well, you want to identify obviously a challenging research problem that's related to databases, which generally means storage, indexing, queries, and updates over large, persistent shared data.

19
00:02:21,422 --> 00:02:24,240
Then you got to come up with a novel solution, of course.

20
00:02:25,530 --> 00:02:35,760
gotta show that it works, have experiments that proves where it really works, and ideally show where it doesn't, although not everybody is so diligent about that.

21
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:42,212
Um, but it's oh and then finally have a success story where it actually made a difference to somebody somewhere.

22
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:46,240
Um, so where do these problems come from?

23
00:02:46,926 --> 00:02:52,080
And I claim there's actually a short list that covers the gamut.

24
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,760
This is for systems problems now.

25
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:58,640
There are many database problems that are not related to systems, they're more user-oriented.

26
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:03,396
But at the systems level, one thing that drives the agenda is hardware mechanisms.

27
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:15,040
Every time a new hardware mechanism comes along, often not targeted for databases in particular, um, you know, we all look at it and say, gee, that looks like something that we could make use of somehow.

28
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:17,280
We should figure out how best to use it.

29
00:03:17,678 --> 00:03:20,644
Multi-core vector processing, et cetera, et cetera.

30
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:25,348
Umftware mechanism has shown up.

31
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:37,600
again, not necessarily targeting databases, but we start looking and seeing how we might leverage that mechanism to solve some well known problems in databases.

32
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:43,524
So distributed hash table, machine learning, blockchain, log structured storage, and so on.

33
00:03:45,902 --> 00:03:53,696
Third source of problems is a new systems platform, which is not just a mechanism, but just a whole way of organizing computing.

34
00:03:53,936 --> 00:04:00,336
So cloud computing is an obvious example, or specifically cloud storage, very large main memories.

35
00:04:00,496 --> 00:04:09,540
Now people are talking about cloud fog edge, where you some in-network processing in addition to the cloud processors and the edge processors.

36
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:16,612
Each of those is a cause to rethink some of your solutions for data management.

37
00:04:17,454 --> 00:04:21,055
Then there's the possibility of a new workload, a new usage scenario.

38
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:28,164
You know, you're going to train ML models, or you have graph algorithms over big data for knowledge bases or data science.

39
00:04:28,416 --> 00:04:31,056
So that's another source of problems.

40
00:04:31,136 --> 00:04:35,456
And then finally, you optimize for a new metric.

41
00:04:35,536 --> 00:04:51,172
You know, maybe instead of latency or throughput, which are the usual ones for performance, maybe scalability, scaling out or minimizing cost um best you can within um within some performance envelope, and so on.

42
00:04:52,622 --> 00:04:55,972
so the interesting thing about this list is I think it's complete.

43
00:04:57,486 --> 00:05:07,376
I think that pretty much every systems-oriented database research that you find will be motivated by one or more of these bullets.

44
00:05:07,936 --> 00:05:15,428
and it's also kind of useful for grad students and others just to try to think about what it is they're trying to accomplish.

45
00:05:17,166 --> 00:05:23,076
now obviously, in order to make it research, um, you got to claim novelty.

46
00:05:24,494 --> 00:05:26,656
and unfortunately, there's not much new in the world.

47
00:05:26,816 --> 00:05:36,996
Back when I started doing research in the 70s, there was a lot of low-hanging fruit, so many problems that nobody had ever even thought of before.

48
00:05:38,830 --> 00:05:42,416
and so there was plenty of opportunity to innovate.

49
00:05:42,496 --> 00:05:45,056
It's not so easy, and now very often.

50
00:05:45,136 --> 00:06:07,056
when you look at solutions to database systems, problems, you find people are are repackaging versions of salute techniques, like the ones listed here, in a different combination, in a different way, on a different hardware platform, using a different computing platform, et cetera, et cetera.

51
00:06:07,136 --> 00:06:12,068
But it's it's um it's coming up with a genuinely new technique is rare.

52
00:06:12,656 --> 00:06:22,148
And it's really where, I mean, that's these days that's the kind of thing that wins Turing awards, you know, as you come up with a really different way of solving.

53
00:06:22,576 --> 00:06:36,656
It does happen periodically, you know, you see blockchain show up or or um consistent hashing or you know um enclaves in in hardware for for um for highly secure computing and so on.

54
00:06:36,736 --> 00:06:42,816
So they do appear from time to time, but they're not a dime a dozen.

55
00:06:44,238 --> 00:06:46,816
Now I work in an industrial research environment.

56
00:06:46,896 --> 00:07:00,548
I used to be a professor for some years, and but I've been in industry now for more than 30 years, and in industry the time horizon tends to be shorter than in academia.

57
00:07:01,216 --> 00:07:04,176
Um more like say five years.

58
00:07:04,334 --> 00:07:08,576
You know, sometimes we work even shorter, just a little bit ahead of a product group.

59
00:07:08,736 --> 00:07:20,004
But um, when we see problems that we think are more than you know, more like 10 years out, we say that's a that's a great thing for a university to work on, but nobody can predict where the business is going to be in 10 years.

60
00:07:20,256 --> 00:07:22,756
So we try to work a little closer in.

61
00:07:23,536 --> 00:07:28,976
Um you want there to be a big opportunity for revenue or cost saving.

62
00:07:29,056 --> 00:07:41,616
That doesn't mean you're going to succeed, but if you can't even tell a good story as to why the company ought to be interested in the work you're doing, then it's unclear why it should be paid for by the company.

63
00:07:41,696 --> 00:07:43,696
And again, not just something that's

64
00:07:48,112 --> 00:07:49,312
more academic.

65
00:07:50,432 --> 00:07:53,952
and our goal here is to de-risk the solution.

66
00:07:54,112 --> 00:08:01,872
I mean, that it's often the engineers building stuff are just as smart as we are, but the thing they don't have is time.

67
00:08:01,998 --> 00:08:06,112
I mean, they've got to be working on something that's going to ship in the next year or so.

68
00:08:07,232 --> 00:08:16,740
and if they can't put the work on a schedule because they don't know how long it's going to take, then it's really hard for them.

69
00:08:17,152 --> 00:08:27,044
And so de-risking the solution is a big deal, producing a prototype that shows that it actually can be built and they can visualize how long it's going to take to do it.

70
00:08:28,494 --> 00:08:37,347
ideally, you get the product group involved early on because impact is important and if they're invested early, the more likely that they'll be interested in using it.

71
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:54,788
And then finally, since we're a research lab, Microsoft Research, we want to publish it both for public recognition, but also kind of to prove that it was research and not something that the product guys should have done and it was not really research.

72
00:08:55,712 --> 00:08:58,912
so that's where I'm coming from in all of this.

73
00:08:59,246 --> 00:09:19,152
And so, in some sense, the work that I'm going to be talking about for the rest of the seminar about Orleans, is really a case study in a particular data point in this model, if you will, of database research in the industrial sector.

74
00:09:19,438 --> 00:09:30,532
So the research problem that we chose was how to add database features to a cloud programming framework, which is Orleans.

75
00:09:31,312 --> 00:09:35,072
And first question is why is it research?

76
00:09:35,472 --> 00:09:41,072
people have added, I mean, every programming language has some ability to do database management.

77
00:09:41,152 --> 00:09:42,832
What makes this one research?

78
00:09:43,312 --> 00:09:57,636
well, the first is that we're doing it on the cloud computing platform, in particular with cloud storage, and cloud storage has very different characteristics than a disk attached to your server where you're running your program.

79
00:09:58,752 --> 00:10:02,112
secondly, is that we're running a different workload.

80
00:10:02,192 --> 00:10:06,980
We're doing state we stateful cloud services, and I'll explain what I mean by that in a moment.

81
00:10:07,632 --> 00:10:16,992
Um, and finally, um the metrics here are scope are in addition to good performance, it has to scale out because we're going to run on large numbers of servers.

82
00:10:17,152 --> 00:10:27,492
It's got to be robust, it's got to be something that if the workload changes a little bit or the platform changes a little bit, you know, failures, it still works fine.

83
00:10:28,032 --> 00:10:38,276
Um and and finally, programmability, ease of use was a big deal in the development of Orleans for the means of use for the developers.

84
00:10:39,342 --> 00:10:45,572
So let's move forward and see what the problem is in a little more detail and how we went about solving it.

85
00:10:46,158 --> 00:10:53,552
So I said that we're interested in stateful application services.

86
00:10:53,792 --> 00:11:05,472
So the service has to be maintaining some kind of state information, Internet of Things, games, social networking, mobile applications, telemetry.

87
00:11:07,632 --> 00:11:16,832
just by the description of the of the of the application type, you can easily imagine that there's a lot of state information that has to be maintained in order to offer the service.

88
00:11:19,726 --> 00:11:23,428
and so that's the style of application.

89
00:11:25,678 --> 00:11:33,536
And I probably should stop here and just say that from now on if you want to interrupt with questions, I'm all for you know, raise your hand, speak up.

90
00:11:33,856 --> 00:11:41,376
you might have your mic disabled, so you probably have to raise your hand in order to speak, and one of us will will um will turn on your mic for you.

91
00:11:41,776 --> 00:11:41,936
Sure.

92
00:11:42,096 --> 00:11:52,804
So one protocol we can follow is if you have questions, you can raise your hand or you can type in your question in the chat window, and I'm going to monitor both.

93
00:11:53,646 --> 00:11:54,948
Okay, great, great.

94
00:11:55,342 --> 00:11:57,060
Happy to make this interactive.

95
00:11:57,376 --> 00:11:59,696
So that's the style of application.

96
00:11:59,776 --> 00:12:06,468
And if you look at that list, that's like a that is a large fraction of the new applications that are being developed these days.

97
00:12:08,016 --> 00:12:11,748
not um you could cover covers quite a wide gamut.

98
00:12:13,936 --> 00:12:20,068
in all of these applications that I just listed, application state is usually represented as objects.

99
00:12:21,070 --> 00:12:30,736
And the reason is that in all these cases, the state information is usually the state of some object in the real world.

100
00:12:30,976 --> 00:12:39,456
Um, you know, the state of a device or the state of a game or the you know the state of a of a um of a mobile device, etc.

101
00:12:39,776 --> 00:12:44,100
So um it's these are naturally object-oriented applications.

102
00:12:46,638 --> 00:12:58,336
in a game, you might have you can have players, games, grid positions, lobbies, player profiles, leaderboards, money, in-game money, weapons caches, if it's that kind of game.

103
00:12:58,896 --> 00:13:06,656
so you know you can easily see that there's a lot of different kinds of objects that are being maintained as state.

104
00:13:06,736 --> 00:13:12,324
And so it's really natural to implement these applications as in an object-oriented way.

105
00:13:14,190 --> 00:13:26,176
in the IoT area, for example, where you've got a lot of sensors and out there devices, it's clear the things, the internet of things, it's the things, and so those things are objects.

106
00:13:26,496 --> 00:13:45,444
And there's a modeling technique called digital twins, which has become quite popular where you have objects running in the cloud, they're called twins, which are basically surrogates for the object out there in the physical world.

107
00:13:45,934 --> 00:13:54,020
So you've got a thermometer hooked up to a server somewhere, and then you've got its digital twin, which is an object running in the cloud.

108
00:13:55,374 --> 00:14:02,052
and their entire systems built around this to make those digital twin models available to application developers.

109
00:14:04,014 --> 00:14:06,736
the applications themselves have a number of interesting properties.

110
00:14:07,136 --> 00:14:09,060
First of all, they manage millions of objects.

111
00:14:11,214 --> 00:14:14,336
streams, images, videos, knowledge graphs.

112
00:14:14,862 --> 00:14:16,736
there's lots and lots of them.

113
00:14:17,296 --> 00:14:20,228
secondly, is the objects are active for quite a while.

114
00:14:21,006 --> 00:14:25,536
this is not where you just spin up an object and then have it disappear quickly.

115
00:14:25,696 --> 00:14:27,936
Usually they're they're active at least for minutes.

116
00:14:28,016 --> 00:14:36,068
You know, you log in to play a game, and maybe you're playing the game for 20 minutes, and all those objects are around for um for that period.

117
00:14:36,336 --> 00:14:43,616
Um, and and sometimes they're basically as long as the application is up and running, you expect the objects to be active.

118
00:14:43,776 --> 00:14:47,012
For example, digital twins in an IoT application.

119
00:14:47,936 --> 00:14:50,340
the application does heavy computation.

120
00:14:51,216 --> 00:14:55,216
some of it's lightweight interactive stuff, but some of it is really quite heavy.

121
00:14:55,296 --> 00:14:56,416
You do complex actions.

122
00:14:56,576 --> 00:15:04,896
Maybe you're rendering images, you're running heavyweight queries over high-speed streams of data flowing in from the outside world.

123
00:15:04,976 --> 00:15:20,164
Maybe you're running computations over graphs or searching knowledge graph in response to an interaction with with a with an end um with a communication endpoint, a user or some other device.

124
00:15:20,846 --> 00:15:22,976
And finally, there's heavy communication.

125
00:15:23,056 --> 00:15:27,056
There's messages between objects at a pretty high rate.

126
00:15:27,216 --> 00:15:29,376
There's also a high bandwidth message streams.

127
00:15:29,616 --> 00:15:50,000
So this need for lots of objects, lots of computation, and lots of communication leads to a implementation structure where the um, the system is split up into tiers where you have storage.

128
00:15:50,400 --> 00:15:55,520
you've got a middle tier of comp compute, and you've got a front-end communications.

129
00:15:55,760 --> 00:16:00,000
And this enables you to scale out each of these functions independently.

130
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:09,920
So, you know, whereas you know, people build database systems often with server attached storage and stored procedures, and it's all wrapped up in one box.

131
00:16:10,158 --> 00:16:16,080
You know, you the computation to run the query, the storage connected to that box.

132
00:16:16,910 --> 00:16:21,092
but in this environment, you really want to be able to scale this stuff out independently.

133
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:27,920
And so generally the storage is not co-located on the machines where the computation is done.

134
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:36,932
And then similarly, all the front-end chattiness with the outside world is generally done on front-end servers like web servers and like

135
00:16:41,198 --> 00:16:46,948
these sorts of applications are often implemented these days with what's called an actor system.

136
00:16:48,160 --> 00:16:58,340
and the reason why people like actors is that the programming model of actors simplifies distributed programming in this multi-tier architecture.

137
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:00,080
So, what's an actor?

138
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:01,520
Well, it's simply an object.

139
00:17:01,742 --> 00:17:03,520
So why even give it another name?

140
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:07,715
Well, it's it's got it, it's an object, but it has a couple of extra properties.

141
00:17:08,400 --> 00:17:16,900
first of all, the actors communicate only using message passing, there's no shared memory between them.

142
00:17:17,294 --> 00:17:25,060
And this is again it's natural if you think about the objects in the applications that I'm describing, they can't share memory.

143
00:17:25,599 --> 00:17:29,600
they're running on different machines, they're accessing remote storage.

144
00:17:30,560 --> 00:17:34,880
you can't count on them being co-located so that they could share memory.

145
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:38,500
So it's best to just assume they can't share memory.

146
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:45,600
and the actors, each actor is also single-threaded.

147
00:17:45,680 --> 00:17:52,196
It runs one, it processes one message at a time, does the work, and then returns to its caller.

148
00:17:52,750 --> 00:17:56,000
So there's no multi-threaded execution inside of an actor.

149
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:04,720
Now, on today's multi-processors, this sounds like a terrible idea because you know, you've got all this parallelism in the compute uh framework.

150
00:18:04,880 --> 00:18:07,760
You expect multi-threading in order to benefit from it.

151
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:13,028
But but um it's it's for an application developer, it's very hard.

152
00:18:13,326 --> 00:18:22,480
You know, first of all, you've got multi-threads, they're running inside the same process, they're sharing the state of the um the process.

153
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:26,320
Um they trip over each other, one updates with no one reads.

154
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:27,520
This stuff is hard.

155
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:31,440
And for those of you who've done multi-threaded programming, you know it's difficult.

156
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:36,708
And this is really supposed to be an easy-to-use programming environment for applications.

157
00:18:37,774 --> 00:18:41,520
plus, the actors are these fine-grained objects out there in the world.

158
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:45,840
So it's not clear you even need multiple threads running inside the actor.

159
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:51,204
The rate at which they're processing messages is not so high that it can't be handled by a single thread.

160
00:18:51,680 --> 00:18:58,404
So the assumption is that the actors are pretty fine-grained, and therefore it's fine to just have them execute.

161
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:01,188
each actor executes on a single thread.

162
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:06,160
there are many programming environments for actors.

163
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:08,240
they were listed actually on the last page.

164
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:10,640
I should probably have said something about that on the right.

165
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:15,680
There's Erlang, which is probably the most well known of these, it's the oldest.

166
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:26,788
There's a system called ACO, which is basically the Erlang programming model implemented in Java and Orleans, which is the system, the system that I work on.

167
00:19:28,526 --> 00:19:40,576
Orleans is an open source cross-platform framework for actors building robust app distributed applications on the Microsoft platform on.NET.

168
00:19:41,216 --> 00:19:46,148
It's been open source since 2014, I think.

169
00:19:47,456 --> 00:19:53,988
And it's pretty widely used outside Microsoft is very widely used inside Microsoft.

170
00:19:54,670 --> 00:20:12,656
So many of the Microsoft services that you know about, a lot of the games on Xbox, Teams, Skype, fraud detection, service for our application, suite, Office, and our IoT framework.

171
00:20:12,816 --> 00:20:17,696
they all, they all have major subsystems that are built on top of Orleans.

172
00:20:18,350 --> 00:20:31,236
And for anybody who's been involved in the Orleans, those of you at UCI have been doing it, you know from just watching the chat rooms, there are people all over the world that are active at any given time working on this.

173
00:20:32,736 --> 00:20:37,540
The big thing about Orleans is that it invented the virtual actor model.

174
00:20:41,376 --> 00:20:44,004
The idea is that each object has a primary key.

175
00:20:44,976 --> 00:20:46,976
Objects are loaded on demand.

176
00:20:47,296 --> 00:20:54,576
So what that means is you don't you don't use the new operator to create a new instance of an object.

177
00:20:54,736 --> 00:20:58,336
All you do is you just reference the object based on its key.

178
00:20:58,576 --> 00:21:03,268
And if the object is is running, then it's invoked.

179
00:21:04,176 --> 00:21:08,496
If the object is not running, it's loaded and activated on demand.

180
00:21:08,576 --> 00:21:10,256
Basically, the system will,

181
00:21:14,016 --> 00:21:15,776
it will pick a um server.

182
00:21:15,776 --> 00:21:17,296
that's um lightly loaded.

183
00:21:17,376 --> 00:21:18,628
it will, it will.

184
00:21:19,136 --> 00:21:28,816
um, uh, load the object, load its state, um, it'll run its its constructor, um and um and thereby make it available.

185
00:21:28,896 --> 00:21:31,364
and it'll remember where it put the object.

186
00:21:31,982 --> 00:21:34,736
And the object will stay there for for a while.

187
00:21:35,022 --> 00:21:36,896
As long as it's being used, it will stay there.

188
00:21:36,976 --> 00:21:40,336
It'll only be deactivated after an idle period.

189
00:21:40,576 --> 00:21:51,056
So generally, the application developer does not preload the object or it doesn't try to deactivate it manually.

190
00:21:51,136 --> 00:21:54,980
You just let the underlying runtime do this do this for you.

191
00:21:55,776 --> 00:22:01,776
Um and and for scalability, load Orleans will load balance the objects across servers.

192
00:22:01,936 --> 00:22:04,336
Usually what it does is it's just round robin.

193
00:22:04,496 --> 00:22:13,700
