00:45:17,392 --> 00:45:20,032
but what I'm gonna I only have a limited time left.

400
00:45:20,112 --> 00:45:26,032
I'm gonna focus just on transactions, just as an example, to take it down a level of detail.

401
00:45:26,432 --> 00:45:34,432
So um the interface for writing transactions in Orleans is the same as in.NET or Java Enterprise Edition.

402
00:45:34,512 --> 00:45:46,592
And the way it works is that if you um when you define an actor, you tag methods um with um if you want them to run as a transaction.

403
00:45:46,672 --> 00:45:56,992
So if you want the withdraw function on an account to run as a transaction, you set the transaction option attribute to be required.

404
00:45:57,262 --> 00:45:58,756
Transaction is required.

405
00:45:59,072 --> 00:46:05,988
All right, and this goes back to the late 1990s, and it's it's become pretty much the universal way of doing this.

406
00:46:06,190 --> 00:46:16,708
Um it actually came originally from a Microsoft project called Microsoft Transaction Server introduced it in um the late 90s, and everybody's adopted it since then.

407
00:46:17,472 --> 00:46:21,872
Um but there are big performance challenges in doing this in Orleans.

408
00:46:21,952 --> 00:46:34,112
The first is when you build transactions, the first thing you need is a log to keep track of all the updates and all the changes of transaction state from active to committed or aborted.

409
00:46:34,318 --> 00:46:36,356
But in the cloud, there is no log.

410
00:46:37,774 --> 00:46:38,528
I mean, I don't know.

411
00:46:38,768 --> 00:46:45,092
As far as I know, none of the cloud vendors offer log as a service to application developers.

412
00:46:45,488 --> 00:46:47,332
So you got to do this yourself.

413
00:46:48,208 --> 00:47:01,408
The second is the latency and accessing storage is going to be a problem because normally the way you implement these things is you set locks, and if you're going to run the transaction for too long, you're going to be holding the lock for a while.

414
00:47:01,488 --> 00:47:02,852
It's going to create contention.

415
00:47:03,968 --> 00:47:06,608
The third is that objects migrate between servers.

416
00:47:06,688 --> 00:47:12,768
You know, if the server, if an object is deactivated, when it's activated the next time, it might not be in the same server.

417
00:47:12,928 --> 00:47:15,168
So you can't cache state on that server.

418
00:47:15,248 --> 00:47:19,044
You've always got to go to the storage service to load it.

419
00:47:19,968 --> 00:47:27,396
And unlike you know, most distributed transaction systems rely on the fact that most transactions are not distributed.

420
00:47:28,078 --> 00:47:38,884
You know, they can run distributed transactions, but you go to great pains to make sure that most of your transactions run locally on one machine because the distribution is so expensive.

421
00:47:39,248 --> 00:47:45,328
Here, all the transactions are going to be distributed because the the actors are spread out.

422
00:47:46,048 --> 00:47:49,060
You can't cluster them very easily.

423
00:47:49,808 --> 00:47:57,888
So the solution that we came up with is to use a speculative commit operation to hide the storage latency.

424
00:47:58,126 --> 00:47:59,876
I'm going to show you how that works.

425
00:48:01,198 --> 00:48:05,728
So you execute a transaction, I think what you would regard as the obvious way.

426
00:48:05,808 --> 00:48:16,608
You've got a root object here on the right, and it um it starts calling the other objects, you know, remote procedure call to the other objects.

427
00:48:17,088 --> 00:48:28,528
eventually when it hits these objects, it sets locks on the objects that it accesses, standard two-phase locking, like any standard transaction system.

428
00:48:29,168 --> 00:48:36,260
and then at the end, the um when everything returns to the root object, the root object tries to commit.

429
00:48:36,782 --> 00:48:39,876
And that runs this two-phase commit protocol.

430
00:48:40,288 --> 00:48:51,408
Um, and generally in these systems, you have a transaction manager, a separate service that runs the two-phase commit protocol to make sure the transaction is atomic.

431
00:48:51,568 --> 00:48:59,168
By that I mean that either all the objects um commit the data permanently or none of them do.

432
00:48:59,406 --> 00:49:00,356
It's all or nothing.

433
00:49:01,088 --> 00:49:07,248
And in order to do this, the transaction manager has to have a log, it's got to keep track of its state somewhere.

434
00:49:07,408 --> 00:49:11,908
And since there is no server private storage, it has to do this in the cloud.

435
00:49:12,270 --> 00:49:18,052
So that log has got to be in some storage system somewhere in the data center.

436
00:49:19,568 --> 00:49:21,008
So let's see how this works.

437
00:49:21,088 --> 00:49:23,728
So this is the standard way of running two phase commits.

438
00:49:23,808 --> 00:49:35,940
So you got this transaction manager, and you got these three objects that are part of the transaction, and then the transaction manager has storage nearby and in storage service, and then the objects have storage.

439
00:49:36,494 --> 00:49:41,188
So the way it starts is the the transaction manager asks the objects to prepare.

440
00:49:42,190 --> 00:49:46,724
By that it means it's going to store its updates on disk somewhere in storage.

441
00:49:47,502 --> 00:49:54,340
And when it's all done, when it has an acknowledgement that the data is saved to storage, it's going to acknowledge that it's prepared.

442
00:49:55,566 --> 00:50:03,568
And this when the transaction manager gets that acknowledgement from all of the objects, then it proceeds to tell them all to um commit.

443
00:50:03,648 --> 00:50:04,848
So it commits locally.

444
00:50:04,928 --> 00:50:08,292
That's the operation that actually says the transaction is committed.

445
00:50:08,622 --> 00:50:11,008
From here on, you can't back out.

446
00:50:11,214 --> 00:50:12,356
It's got to be committed.

447
00:50:12,878 --> 00:50:16,836
And so it just goes off and it tells all the objects to commit.

448
00:50:17,168 --> 00:50:26,980
And so the object releases its lock now since it knows that its update is permanent and thereby makes it available to other transactions, logs its commit.

449
00:50:27,886 --> 00:50:33,028
In its own storage and returns to the transaction manager.

450
00:50:33,488 --> 00:50:44,528
Now the problem with this is you've got all this back and forth communications and two whoops and two communications with um with storage.

451
00:50:44,688 --> 00:50:47,652
And during that whole period, you're holding this lock.

452
00:50:48,238 --> 00:50:49,648
So that's a long time.

453
00:50:49,808 --> 00:50:57,008
That's you know 10 um tenth a second, 10th of a second, many tenths of a second.

454
00:50:57,390 --> 00:51:03,556
You're gonna get very low throughput on that piece of data because you're holding the lock for so long.

455
00:51:04,014 --> 00:51:10,372
So what we decided to do instead is to release the lock right away.

456
00:51:10,608 --> 00:51:17,924
When that request to prepare shows up at the object, the first thing the object does is it releases its lock before it does anything else.

457
00:51:18,368 --> 00:51:26,660
So there's you don't accept you know you the locking is not gonna incur any of the delay of storage accesses.

458
00:51:27,008 --> 00:51:29,088
And then the rest proceeds as before.

459
00:51:29,168 --> 00:51:38,532
You log the update, you vote prepared, when all the updates, when all the objects agree to prepare, you're gonna log commit, and then you finish up.

460
00:51:39,488 --> 00:51:42,128
So why is this a bad thing?

461
00:51:42,288 --> 00:51:48,288
Well, the problem is that once you release the lock, other transactions now can jump in.

462
00:51:48,448 --> 00:51:48,900
Whoops.

463
00:51:51,728 --> 00:51:59,088
Other transactions can jump in and can start updating this object, even though it's not committed, right?

464
00:52:00,334 --> 00:52:07,168
Until it gets the commit message, it doesn't know whether the transaction is going to be committed because it could abort.

465
00:52:07,408 --> 00:52:13,168
I mean, there could be a crash, one of the other objects could have disappeared and not be willing to prepare.

466
00:52:13,454 --> 00:52:29,476
So once you release this lock, you're running a risk that if another transaction accesses or updates that object, you could end up updating, you could end up reading data that is actually going to be undone, that's gonna actually be thrown out.

467
00:52:30,048 --> 00:52:38,768
And and so in order to make that okay, um, you've got to take a you've got to take what's called a commit dependency.

468
00:52:38,848 --> 00:52:40,448
So let's walk through this now.

469
00:52:40,608 --> 00:52:43,888
I I don't know any, I don't know how to do this in pictures.

470
00:52:44,048 --> 00:52:55,652
So the transaction um right, it sets a lock when it receives the prepare, um it releases the lock and starts its write, as I just showed you.

471
00:52:56,046 --> 00:53:04,484
And now we have the second transaction and it comes in and it reads the object, which in a normal database system would be disallowed.

472
00:53:04,768 --> 00:53:07,968
And what it does is it takes a commit dependency on T1.

473
00:53:08,048 --> 00:53:15,044
And what that means is T2 is not going to commit until that dependency is resolved, which means T1 has already been committed.

474
00:53:15,408 --> 00:53:24,048
So T2 finishes, it receives a prepare, it releases its lock, it can't do anything because T1 is still in flight.

475
00:53:24,174 --> 00:53:25,888
It's got to wait for T1 to finish.

476
00:53:25,998 --> 00:53:26,980
It can't do anything.

477
00:53:27,568 --> 00:53:33,248
But now that T2 released its lock, T3 can go jump in and it can update this object.

478
00:53:33,328 --> 00:53:35,728
And T4 can then update.

479
00:53:35,808 --> 00:53:40,768
You can have a whole bunch of transactions, one after the other, that can read and write this object.

480
00:53:40,928 --> 00:53:48,580
Um, and after it finishes its write, um then all those updates can go out in a batch.

481
00:53:49,358 --> 00:54:01,648
And when it finishes that batch, then it update, then it can acknowledge, it can acknowledge the prepare and and and can go through the commit.

482
00:54:01,808 --> 00:54:14,788
So basically, you're able to batch all of these updates to write them to storage at once, and you're also able to avoid this this um uh this contention.

483
00:54:15,648 --> 00:54:20,388
So um anyway, we get huge performance improvements from this.

484
